+# Forking Base locally
+
+This checkout is Foundry-flavored Scaffold-ETH 2. To start a local fork of Base
+mainnet from the repo root, use:
+
+```sh
+yarn fork -- base
+```
+
+The `--` matters.
+
+## What the old onboarding note got wrong
+
+The old note said to run:
+
+```sh
+yarn fork base
+```
+
+In this repo that does not pass `base` through to the Foundry package's `fork`
+script. It falls back to the script default, `mainnet`, so Anvil forks Ethereum
+mainnet instead of Base mainnet.
+
+The relevant root package scripts are:
+
+```json
+// package.json
+"fork": "yarn foundry:fork",
+"foundry:fork": "yarn workspace @se-2/foundry fork"
+```
+
+The Foundry package then defaults its positional argument to `mainnet`:
+
+```json
+// packages/foundry/package.json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}"
+```
+
+The Makefile uses that value as Anvil's upstream fork URL and also forces the
+local chain id to `31337`:
+
+```make
+# packages/foundry/Makefile
+fork: setup-anvil-wallet
+	anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+The Base RPC alias does exist in Foundry config:
+
+```toml
+# packages/foundry/foundry.toml
+[rpc_endpoints]
+mainnet = "https://eth-mainnet.alchemyapi.io/v2/${ALCHEMY_API_KEY}"
+base = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+```
+
+So the problem is not that `base` is missing. The problem is that `yarn fork
+base` never makes `base` become `FORK_URL`.
+
+I checked the script expansion in this checkout with a harmless `make` wrapper:
+
+```text
+yarn workspace @se-2/foundry fork base  ->  make fork FORK_URL=mainnet
+yarn workspace @se-2/foundry fork -- base  ->  make fork FORK_URL=base
+```
+
+That explains the empty reads from Base USDC at
+`0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913`: on an Ethereum mainnet fork,
+there is no contract at that Base-specific address, so calls to it return empty
+data.
+
+## Why `cast chain-id` did not catch it
+
+`cast chain-id --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545` only checks the local Anvil
+chain id. The Makefile starts every fork with:
+
+```make
+anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+That means an Ethereum mainnet fork and a Base mainnet fork both answer with
+`31337` locally. A chain-id response proves the node is running; it does not
+prove which upstream chain was forked.
+
+## Check the fork by reading Base USDC
+
+After starting the fork with `yarn fork -- base`, check the token we actually
+depend on:
+
+```sh
+cast call \
+  --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545 \
+  0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 \
+  "symbol()(string)"
+```
+
+Expected output:
+
+```text
+"USDC"
+```
+
+This distinguishes a Base fork from an Ethereum mainnet fork: the same address
+has bytecode on Base and is empty on Ethereum mainnet.

